fr:mainmatter.html:p:
In type theory, natural number is defined using peano encoding.
The type \mathbb {N} is formed by the formation rule:

    \frac {}{\vdash \mathbb {N}\space \text {type}}\mathbb {N}\text {-form}

Peano's first axiom postulates the existence of a natural number 0.
The introduction rule for \mathbb {N} has a 0 constructor and a successor function.

    \frac {}{\vdash 0:\mathbb {N}}\mathbb {N}\text {-intro-0}
    \quad 
    \frac {}{\vdash \text {succ}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}} \mathbb {N}\text {-intro-succ}

The elimination rule is actually the type theoretical induction principle of \mathbb {N}:
In order to show that \forall  n:\mathbb {N}.P(n) holds, it suffices to show that P(0) holds and that \forall  n:\mathbb {N}.P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n)) holds.
The type theoretical induction principle is therefore formulated using a type family P over \mathbb {N}:

    \frac {
        \begin {align*}
            \Gamma &,\,n:\mathbb {N}\vdash  P(n)\space \text {type}
            \\
            \Gamma &\vdash  p_0:P(0)
            \\
            \Gamma &\vdash  p_S:\Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n))
        \end {align*}
    }{
        \Gamma \vdash \text {ind}_\mathbb {N}(p_0,p_S):\Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)
    }(\mathbb {N} \text {-ind})

The induction principle tells us what we need to do in order to construct a dependent function \text {ind}_\mathbb {N} of type \Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n).
We might alternatively write the induction principle as:

    \frac {
        \Gamma ,n:\mathbb {N}\vdash  P(n)\space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma \vdash \text {ind}_\mathbb {N}:\left (P(0)\to \left (\Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n))\right )\to \Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\right )
    }(\mathbb {N} \text {-ind})

The computation rule asserts that the dependent function \text {ind}_\mathbb {N} behaves as expected:

    \frac {
        \begin {align*}
            \Gamma &,n:\mathbb {N}\vdash  P(n)\space \text {type}
            \\
            \Gamma &\vdash  p_0:P(0)
            \\
            \Gamma &\vdash  p_S:\Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n))
        \end {align*}
    }{
        \text {ind}_\mathbb {N}(p_0,p_S,m)\equiv 
        \begin {cases}
            p_0&\text {if }m=0
            \\
            p_S(n,\text {ind}_\mathbb {N}(p_0,p_S,n))&\text {if }m=\text {succ}(n)
        \end {cases}
    }(\mathbb {N} \text {-comp})

fr:tree.fr:mainmatter:



    We construct the addition by performing induction over the second variable n.
    That is, to construct an element

        m:\mathbb {N} \vdash  \text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m):\mathbb {N} \to  \mathbb {N}

    The context \Gamma  \equiv  m:\mathbb {N} is fixed.

Therefore we need to construct:

    \begin {align*}
        \Gamma &\vdash  \text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m):\mathbb {N}
        \\
        \Gamma &\vdash  \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m):\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}                
    \end {align*}   

The \text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N} is defined to be identity function trivially. To see how \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N} is defined, we need to perform induction:

    \begin {align*}
        \text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m, \text {succ}(n))&\equiv 
        \text {ind}_\mathbb {N}(\text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m), \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m), \text {succ}(n))
        \\
        &\equiv  \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m,n, \text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m,n))
        \\
        &\equiv  \text {succ}(\text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m,n))
    \end {align*}

Hence \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N} is defined as:

    \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m,n,x) \equiv  \text {succ}(x)

A formal construction of \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N} is as follows:

    \dfrac {
        \dfrac {
            \dfrac {
                \dfrac {}{\vdash \mathbb {N}\space \text {type}}
                \quad 
                \dfrac {
                    \dfrac {}{\vdash  \mathbb {N}\space \text {type}}
                    \quad 
                    \dfrac {}{\vdash  \text {succ}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}}
                }{
                    n:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {succ}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}
                }
            }{
                m:\mathbb {N}, n:\mathbb {N}\vdash  \text {succ}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}
            } 
        }{
            m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \lambda  n.\text {succ} : \mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N})
        }
    }{
        m:\mathbb {N}\vdash  \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m):\equiv \lambda  m.\text {succ} : \mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N})
    }\text {Block-1}

Finally we combine the derivation together:

    \dfrac {
        \dfrac {
            \dfrac {
                \dfrac {
                    \vdash  \mathbb {N}\space \text {type}
                }{
                    m:\mathbb {N}\vdash  m:\mathbb {N}
                }
            }{
                m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m):\equiv  m:\mathbb {N}
            }
            \quad  
            \dfrac {\text {Block-1}}{
                m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m):\mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N})
            }
        }{
            m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {ind}_\mathbb {N} (\text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m), \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m)):\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}
        }
    }{
        m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m):\equiv \text {ind}_\mathbb {N} (\text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m), \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m)):\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}
    }

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Recall the definition of addition function \text {add}: \mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}) satisfying the specification:

    \begin {align*}
        m + 0 & :\equiv  m
        \\ 
        m + \text {succ}(n) & :\equiv  \text {succ}(m + n)
    \end {align*}

Such definition is enough to characterize the addition function.
Because it postulates te behavior of \text {add}_\mathbb {N} at the constructor of \mathbb {N}

More generally, we can define a dependent function f:\Pi  n:\mathbb {N}.P(n) by induction on n using

    \begin {align*}
        p_0 & : P(0)
        \\
        p_S & : \Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n))
    \end {align*}

Just present the definition by writing

    \begin {align*}
        f(0) & :\equiv  p_0
        \\
        f(\text {succ}(n)) & :\equiv  p_S(n,f(n))
    \end {align*}

f is said to be defined by pattern matching on the variable n.
